  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is a robot joint?",
      "a": "A joint is where two parts of the arm meet and move. Most robot arm joints rotate, turned by an electric motor through a gearbox, and six of them give the arm full freedom to reach and point.",
      "asks": [
        "How many joints does a robot arm have?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is an end effector?",
      "a": "It is whatever is fitted on the end of the robot arm to do the work: grippers, suction cups, welding torches, paint guns or screwdrivers. Changing it lets one arm do many jobs.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a robot hand called?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a robot arm know where to move?",
      "a": "The controller uses the arm's lengths and geometry to calculate each joint angle needed to reach a target, called inverse kinematics. Encoders measure the real joint angles so it can correct any error.",
      "asks": [
        "What is inverse kinematics?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is an actuator?",
      "a": "An actuator is the part that makes something move. In robot arms it is usually an electric servo motor with a gearbox, though some very strong robots use hydraulic or air-powered cylinders.",
      "asks": [
        "What moves a robot's joints?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does six degrees of freedom mean?",
      "a": "It means six independent ways to move. To place a tool anywhere and tilt it any way, you need three for position, up-down, left-right and forward-back, and three for its angle.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do robot arms have six axes?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Unimate, the first industrial robot, began work at a General Motors plant in 1961.",
    "Most industrial robot arms have six rotating joints."
  ],
